# POSIX-mode vacuous-green census roster — the NEGATIVES of scripts/posix-vacuous-green-census.sh.
#
# Format: <path>|<sh_exit_code>|<class>|<measured_on>|<note>
# One line per scripts/*.sh that contains `<(`, is NOT interpreter-guarded, and is
# NOT comment-only — i.e. every file the census would otherwise red. An entry is a
# claim that the file was RUN under /bin/sh and did NOT produce a vacuous green.
#
# Classes:
#   truncated-red  the POSIX parse error killed it; the caller reads a non-zero exit
#   refused-red    it refused for its own reason (config/usage/env) before the `<(`
#   completed-red  it ran to completion under sh and exited non-zero, stdout byte-
#                  identical to a `bash` control
#   honest-green   it ran to completion under sh and exited 0, and a `bash` control
#                  proves nothing was skipped (differences, if any, are quoted)
#
# `honest-green` is the only class that may carry exit code 0, and it REQUIRES the
# bash-control note. Anything measured to exit 0 while truncated does not belong on
# this roster at all — it gets the interpreter guard instead.
#
# MEASURED 2026-09-11 on origin/main 038d310fce9e65b543e333cfd87d2db23634db60,
# macOS /bin/sh = bash 3.2.57(1) in POSIX mode, by RUNNING each file, not by
# grepping it (task cch-w53-fu-posix-vacuous-green-census-across-scripts).
# Seven network/ledger scripts were run with a stub PATH (no-op bp/gh/psql/curl/
# createdb/dropdb) so no write could escape; that is noted per line.
